      "target": "../sections/sec-000-json-schema-creation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1st Felix Neubauer\n, 3rd Benjamin Uekermann\nInstitute for Parallel and Distributed Systems\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nStuttgart, Germany\nFelix.Neubauer@ipvs.uni-stuttgart.de,\nBenjamin.Uekermann@ipvs.uni-stuttgart.de\n2nd J¨urgen Pleiss\nInstitute of Biochemistry and Technical Biochemistry\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nStuttgart, Germany\nJuergen.Pleiss@itb.uni-stuttgart.de\nAbstract—Model-Driven Engineering (MDE) places models\nat the core of system and data engineering processes. In the\ncontext of research data, these models are typically expressed\nas schemas that define the structure and semantics of data",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Authors and abstract: Felix Neubauer and Benjamin Uekermann (Institute for Parallel and Distributed Systems) and Jürgen Pleiss (Institute of Biochemistry and Technical Biochemistry), University of Stuttgart. In model-driven engineering (MDE), research-data models are usually schemas, and many domains lack standard ones that non-experts can create. The authors combine large language models (LLMs) with deterministic techniques for creating and modifying JSON Schemas and for schema mapping from natural-language input, built into the open-source tool MetaConfigurator, which already offers visual editing, validation, code generation and form generation. LLMs generate mapping rules from heterogeneous JSON, CSV, XML and YAML data, and the rules are executed deterministically for scalability and reliability. A chemistry example demonstrates the approach. The block is headed \"JSON Schema Creation\" but holds the front matter, abstract and index terms.",
