      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 1,
      "pageEnd": 1,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Model-Driven Engineering (MDE) emphasizes the central\nrole of models in the design, development, and operation of\nsystems [1]. In the context of research data management [2]–\n[4], models are typically expressed as schemas, which define\nthe structure and semantics of datasets. These models enable\nkey functionalities such as instance validation, transformation,\ncode generation, and visualization, and are essential for ensur-\ning data quality, interoperability, and reusability.\nDespite their importance, many scientific domains still\nlack standardized data models. In fields such as chemistry,\ndata",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduction. Schemas enable validation, transformation, code generation and visualization and underpin data quality and interoperability, yet fields such as chemistry often keep data in electronic lab notebooks or spreadsheets with implicit meaning, and flat CSV cannot express relationships or constraints. LLMs can turn natural-language descriptions into models, but used alone they give no guarantee of validity, produce hard-to-interpret plain text, struggle with large datasets and need well-crafted prompts. The authors therefore extend MetaConfigurator with LLM-based schema creation, modification and querying guarded by prompt engineering, context management, post-processing and validation (Section III-A), and with data integration in which LLMs write human-readable mapping rules from JSON, YAML, XML or CSV to a target schema that are then executed deterministically (Section III-B). Section IV applies this to chemistry Excel data. The block also carries the DFG funding numbers (528693298, 358283783, 390740016).",
