      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "JSON and JSON Schema",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-json-and-json-schema.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "JSON is a widespread data format for exchanging infor-\nmation with web services, storing semi-structured documents\nin NoSQL databases [9], and representing structured content\nin APIs and configuration files. Its simplicity and human-\nreadability, combined with wide support across programming\nlanguages, make it a natural fit for data interchange and\nmachine learning pipelines.\nJSON Schema, the de-facto standard for describing the\nstructure and constraints of JSON documents [10], plays a\ncrucial role in enabling validation, interoperability, and au-\ntomation. It can also be understood as a model",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sections II-A and II-B. JSON is widely used for web-service exchange, NoSQL documents, APIs and configuration files, and JSON Schema, the de facto standard for describing JSON structure and constraints, can be read as a modelling language much like class diagrams in MDE. Existing schema editors (MetaConfigurator, Adamant, the paid Liquid Studio editor) all need some understanding of JSON Schema. The authors build on MetaConfigurator because of its modular architecture, its code generation in 17 languages and documentation generation, and because it is open source, free, available as a web service and familiar to them. Schema inference or discovery derives a schema from instances. Chat services such as ChatGPT can write schemas but do not validate or visualize them, and LLMs are distracted by irrelevant context, lose accuracy on low-probability inputs and reason worse on longer inputs; Mior fine-tuned an LLM for schema tasks that clearly outperformed Code Llama.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
