      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Schema Matching and Mapping",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-schema-matching-and-mapping.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Integrating data from various sources and in different for-\nmats poses a significant challenge [20]–[22]. In this work,\nwe study the task of schema mapping: converting an instance\nfrom one JSON schema to another. A schema mapping can\ncontain simple property-to-property correspondences or also\nmore expressive logics. JSON to JSON transformation (Jolt)3,\nis a Java library to transform JSON documents. JSONata4,\n2https://www.liquid-technologies.com/json-schema-editor, acc. 25/06/02\n3https://github.com/bazaarvoice/jolt, acc. 25/06/02\n4https://github.com/jsonata-js/jsonata, acc. 25/06/02\nis another",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Section II-C, schema matching and mapping. The paper studies schema mapping, converting an instance of one JSON Schema into another, which may involve simple property correspondences or richer logic. Tools include the Java library Jolt, the TypeScript library JSONata and the query language jq, which the authors judge unsuited to large documents because it processes in memory with little streaming or parallel support. Schema matching, identifying corresponding elements, has automated approaches surveyed by Rahm and Bernstein for relational databases; Stanek and Killough generate JSON-to-JSON conversion code; and Buss and colleagues use LLMs for relational schema mapping and argue that data integration needs a human in the loop.",
