      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "AI-assisted Schema Matching and Mapping",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-ai-assisted-schema-matching-and-mapping.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Schema mapping is performed using human-defined map-\nping rules or scripts, traditional automated approaches [23],\n[25] and recently also using LLMs [26]. We propose and im-\nplement a new approach, which transforms a JSON document\nto satisfy a given target schema by first generating schema\nmapping rules with the help of a LLM and then executing\nthese deterministically. Aside from JSON, MetaConfigurator\nsupports the data formats YAML and XML and has a function\nto import CSV documents (by conversion to JSON). Therefore,\ndata from any of these formats are supported and can be\ntransformed using th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Section III-B, AI-assisted schema matching and mapping, with the prompts of Figures 1 and 2. A JSON document is transformed to fit a target schema by having an LLM generate mapping rules that are then executed deterministically; YAML, XML and imported CSV are supported through conversion. The mapping language is JSONata. The pipeline infers a source schema from the input with jsonhero/schema-infer, then builds a prompt of instructions (persona, task, detailed rules and a JSONata specification), an input-output example and the user input. Large inputs are shortened by recursively trimming arrays to n items and object properties to 8n, with n starting at 64 and halving until the document is under 64 KB or n reaches 2; the schema inferred from the full document is included to limit information loss, which also caps the document complexity supported. The response is stripped of code fences, checked for syntax (not semantics) with the JSONata library, shown in an editor, and can be applied only once valid; Figure 3 gives an example.",
        "state": "draft",
