      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Application Example",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-application-example.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Chemists have performed chemical synthesis experiments\nand tracked their inputs and results in an Excel table. They\nwant to apply machine learning and other algorithms on their\ndata, but it is not yet in a suitable format10. MetaConfigurator\nis used to convert the excel table into a JSON document\nand automatically infer the corresponding schema. The JSON\ndocument and schema, however, are not yet of sufficient\nquality. Among other points, the product_purity values\nof yes and no have been inferred as being of type string\ninstead of boolean and the ligand and metal_salt\nproperties are all listed",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Section IV, the chemistry application example, with the text of Figure 3. Chemists' synthesis experiments kept in an Excel table are converted to JSON with an inferred schema, which is of poor quality: product_purity values \"yes\" and \"no\" are typed as strings, and ligand and metal_salt properties sit flat on each experiment. The chat interface produces a better schema (Figures 1–2), and automated mapping converts yes/no to booleans and flat paths to nested ones. Figure 3's example maps a document with mof_id and a reagents list to a target with materialId, metal and linkers using an LLM-generated JSONata expression, producing \"ZrCl4\" as the metal and [\"BDC\", \"DABCO\"] as linkers. To target the Chemical Description Language XDL 2.0, which has documentation but no formal schema, the authors paste its documentation into the schema-creation prompt, then, since JSONata cannot map such different structures, generate Python classes for both schemas and write the conversion code themselves.",
